The brief facts of the present case are that on 16.06.2011, the Food Inspector Sh. Raj Pal Singh,
Field Assistant Sh.Pratap Raj under the supervision of Local Health Authority/SDM Sh. B.M.
Mishra reached at the premises M/s Pal Dairy at Arya Samaj Road, Subzi Mandi, Narela, Delhi - 40 where accused/appellant was found conducting business of various food articles which were lying stored for sale for human consumption ;
- that Food Inspector disclosed his identity and expressed his intention to purchase a sample of 'Ghee' from the vendor which was lying in open container ( drum ) bearing no label declaration, to which he agreed ;
- that thereafter as per the prescribed procedure under the PFA CA no. 110/2017 Rajpal Singh Vs.
Delhi Admn/Food Inspector Pages 2 of 10 Act and Rules after giving notice to the vendor/appellant and offer towards the sale price of sample to which he agreed and accepted the sale consideration,
sample was then lifted ; that each sample was separately packed, fastened, marked, sealed and necessary documents including Notice as per Form-VI in the presence of appellant/accused alongwith Panchnama etc was prepared at the spot ; the price of the sample commodity was paid to the vendor ;
- that thereafter one counterpart of sample was sent to Public Analyst (PA) in intact condition and other two counterparts were deposited with SDM/LHA ;
- that vide report dated 06.07.2011, PA found the sample to be adulterated not conforming to the standards on the grounds that the BR reading exceeded the prescribed maximum limit ( it was 46 when the range should be 40.0 to 43.0), the RM value was less than the prescribed minimum limit (
it was 21.47 when it should not be less than 28) and the BT Test ( Baudouin Test) was positive (
should be negative in ghee ) ;
- that upon receipt of report, SDM/LHA directed investigation which was carried out by Food Inspector and after completion of investigation sanction under Section 20 of the PFA Act was obtained from the Director PFA.

The complaint was then filed in the court on 27.09.2011 alleging violation of Section 2(ia),(b)(c) and (m) of PFA Act & Rules 44(C) as punishable under Section 7/16(1)(a) of PFA. Act.
- that the complaint was filed in writing by a public servant, hence, recording of pre-summoning evidence was dispensed with ;
CA no. 110/2017 Rajpal Singh Vs.

- After filing appearance appellant/accused adopted the procedure available as per the statute ;
pre-charge evidence was recorded ; and on the basis of testimony of prosecution witnesses, charge Raj Pal Singh vs State ( Delhi Admn) on 18 October, 2021 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/2371222/
2 was framed against the accused to which he pleaded not guilty ; thereafter statement of accused was recorded and after seeking opportunity to lead DE, no witness was examined or produced ; final arguments were heard and accused was held guilty by the learned trial court observing that the ghee lying in possession of accused meant for sale for human consumption was adulterated and it stands established by prosecution beyond reasonable doubt that the accused had sold adulterated food in violation of Section 2(ia)(a) and (m) of PFA Act and has committed the offence punishable under Section 7/16(1)(a) of PFA Act and was thus convicted for the said offences by the learned trial court.

